Unreleased
    * Plugin options: declared in plugin.toml's [plugin-options], given
      in plugins.enabled, dependencies or plugin load NAME OPTION=VALUE;
      seen as LUISH_PLUGIN_OPTIONS and sh::plugin_options()
    * __luish_cache blocks of plugins that config.toml enables (or that
      an rc.d file loads) are cached with their own keys
    * Fixed: an empty table in plugins.enabled (std.completion = { })
      enables the plugin, as "*" does, instead of nothing
    * Variable tracing: setopt vars.trace (or luish -o vars.trace), then
      where NAME shows where it was set; vars.trace_history keeps the
      last changes, which where -a shows (numbered, in colour)
    * Fixed: test FILE -nt MISSING (and MISSING -ot FILE) is true, as in
      POSIX and dash; [[ ... ]] too, as in bash
    * jobs -i: a menu of the jobs, to bring one to the foreground,
      continue, stop or kill it (K, then the signal; e sends TERM, then
      KILL after 5 s)

Version 0.4.0 2026-10-04 by luispedro
    * plugin: coloured output (plugin.* styles) and more feedback: load,
      unload and add say what they did; sync, update and check list each
      source, with its plugins and a compare link; check takes -q
    * ${x:A} and !$:A no longer take quadratic time on long paths
    * Fixed: typeset -f and savestate printing ${x/}, ${`cmd`},
      >file for and `cmd \\`
    * Fixed: ${a['"'} is a bad substitution (as in dash), not a syntax
      error, and a subscript ends where it ends read unquoted (as in
      zsh); deeply nested ${a[${a[...}} no longer take exponential time
    * Deeply nested $(( that isn't arithmetic no longer takes exponential
      time
    * std/completion: luish's built-ins (options; style's names, values
      and colour schemes)
    * install.sh offers to write the recommended configuration, with
      luish-extra (--config, --no-config), and uses colours
    * Here-strings: cmd <<< word, as in bash and zsh
    * Terminal integration: prompt marks (OSC 133) and the current
      directory (OSC 7); setopt terminal.no_integration turns it off
    * std/notify: a desktop notification when a long command ends
    * clipcopy: copies to the clipboard through the terminal (OSC 52)
    * In vi mode, the cursor's shape shows the input mode
    * Styles: undercurl, underdouble, underdotted, underdashed and ul:COLOUR
    * File names in interactive error messages are links (OSC 8)
    * A colour scheme can set the terminal's own colours (background,
      text, cursor, palette), put back when it no longer applies or the
      shell exits; [style] terminal-colors = false keeps them
      (written as #rrggbb, which Konsole needs)
    * %[style:NAME] and %[style_off] in prompts: named styles, as the
      line editor's
    * The terminal's background colour is detected, to choose between
      dark and light colour schemes (style --detect asks again)
    * setopt highlight.paths: highlighting marks words that name files
    * Highlighting marks syntax errors, from the error to the end of the
      line (style `error`)
    * plugin load -c CODE NAME: a plugin from Rhai code, without a file
    * Styles and colour schemes for the line editor: the `style`
      built-in, and [style] and [colorscheme.NAME] tables in config.toml
      and plugin.toml, replacing $LUISH_HIGHLIGHT
    * A colour scheme can be a dark/light pair, chosen by
      $LUISH_BACKGROUND or $COLORFGBG
    * setopt editor.no_highlight turns off highlighting
    * Highlighting shows exported, array and read-only variables, and
      functions and aliases defined earlier on the line
    * Highlighting tells kinds of command apart (built-in, function,
      alias, external, precommand), and marks options, escapes, special
      parameters, process substitution, tildes, brace expansion and globs
    * More colour names in %F{...}: bright-red and the like
    * zsh's modifiers in parameter expansion: ${x:h}, ${x:t}, ${x:r},
      ${x:e}, ${x:a}, ${x:A}, ${x:u}, ${x:l}, ${x:h2}
    * :a and :A modifiers (and counts) in glob qualifiers, :a and :A in
      history expansion
    * BASH_SOURCE, so ${BASH_SOURCE:A:h} is the script's directory
    * FUNCNAME, BASH_LINENO and caller, as in bash
    * Error messages name the file of the code that failed (not $0), and
      show the call stack
    * Error messages show the text of the failing line
    * Error messages show the text of each call in the stack
    * Functions from the startup cache or a saved state keep their lines
      for error messages
    * config.toml: [env], [env.interactive] and [vars] set variables, and
      [path] adds directories to PATH (before = [...], after = [...])
    * Plugins of a collection load as SOURCE/NAME (std/completion), so
      their names don't clash
    * Collections can have sub-collections: SOURCE/SUB/NAME, or
      SOURCE.SUB.NAME in TOML
    * "/PATH" = "*" in plugin.toml: a plugin of the same source
    * Brace expansion, `a{b,c}` and `{1..10}`, with `setopt expand.braces`
    * Array slices, `${a[i..j]}`, as in Python
    * `plugin add` refuses a source without plugins, and directories
      without a plugin's files are no longer taken for plugins
    * Extensions' precmd, preexec and exit hooks
    * `plugin run FILE|-c CODE [ARG...]` runs Rhai code once
    * `typeset -f` and the saved state no longer turn `$` before
      backquotes into `$$`
    * An array index of 64 Mi or more is an error instead of aborting
      the shell
    * `!{}` in history expansion is an error instead of a crash
    * `%NG` in a prompt counts at most 65536 columns, so a huge N no
      longer runs out of memory
    * History expansion longer than 1 MiB (`!# !# !#`...) is an error
      instead of running out of memory

Version 0.3.0 2026-10-01 by luispedro
    * LUISH_VERSION and LUISH_PATCHLEVEL (as zsh's ZSH_VERSION and
      ZSH_PATCHLEVEL), MACHTYPE, HOSTTYPE and OSTYPE
    * install.sh no longer reads LUISH_VERSION (use --version)
    * The right prompt (RPROMPT, RPS1, RPROMPT2), as in zsh, with
      ZLE_RPROMPT_INDENT and `setopt prompt.transient_rprompt`
    * `__luish_cache` blocks in $ENV, luishrc and the rc.lsh of plugins
      they load are cached too, in a cache of their own; check-cache
      takes `startup` as well as `rc` and `login`
    * The startup cache has an entry per file of rc.d and login.d, keyed
      on PATH and HOME: a changed file reruns only it and the files
      after it, and PATH=$HOME/bin:$PATH adds to the PATH of each shell
    * `__luish_cache env=(...) files=(...) { ... }` blocks cache part of
      a startup file, keyed on the variables and files they list
    * check-cache reports differences by file and block
    * The startup cache saves variables that the files set to the value
      they already had
    * Library plugins (`library = true` in plugin.toml), which
      `plugin list-available` hides unless given `-a`; a directory
      with only plugin.toml is a plugin
    * `print`, as in zsh, in interactive shells (`__luish_internal
      print` anywhere), with `-P` for prompt expansion
    * `sh::expand_prompt(text)` expands `%` sequences for extensions
    * `sh::matches(pattern, text)` matches shell patterns for extensions
    * ssh completion: `Include` takes `?` and `[...]` in patterns, and
      `*` no longer matches files that start with `.`
    * git completion: ranges (`HEAD^..`) no longer fail, and `git diff
      REV ` offers the tracked files
    * `sh::capture` takes the program and its arguments as an array,
      with stdin from /dev/null and stderr discarded, inherited, merged
      or returned; `sh::capture_sh` runs shell code, as `sh::capture` did
    * `sh::which(name)` finds a program as the shell does, and
      `sh::commands(prefix)` lists the executables in PATH
    * `fs::realpath(path)` resolves every symbolic link in a path
    * `umount` completes mount points with a newline or backslash in them
    * History expansion (`!!`, `!$`, `^old^new` ...), with
      `setopt history.expand`, and `history.verify`
    * Process substitution, `<(cmd)` and `>(cmd)`
    * Rhai extensions can no longer use `eval`
    * On its first run, a menu offers to write a config.toml with the
      recommended settings, or with a personal plugin
    * `plugin add`: adds a plugin (a GitHub repository, git or file
      URL, or path) to `config.toml`, after asking, and fetches it
    * Plugins can add commands: `sh::builtin(name, fn)` registers a
      built-in written in Rhai, and `sh::read_line()` reads its input
    * `type` names the plugin that added a built-in
    * std.completion completes about 160 more commands: luish, sh, bash
      and zsh, find, sed, awk, jq, rg, less, compressors, systemctl,
      journalctl, loginctl, ps, top, mount, tmux, curl, wget, ip, gpg,
      openssl, cargo, rustup, go, gcc, cmake, gdb, python, vim, nano, pip,
      uv, conda, pixi, npm, yarn, pnpm, apt, dpkg, dnf, rpm, pacman,
      zypper, apk, brew, snap, flatpak ...
    * std.completion asks Cobra programs (gh, docker, kubectl ...), Click
      programs (black, flask ...) and nix for their completions;
      `complete-cobra PROG...` and `complete-click PROG...` add more
    * std.git-completion is now part of std.completion
    * `import` in an extension's module is relative to that module's file
    * `import "@SOURCE/PLUGIN/MODULE"` imports a module of another plugin,
      such as std's completion engine (`@std/completion/lib`)

Version 0.2.0 2026-09-29 by luispedro
    * Fix crashes on Tab after a word like `--opt=value`, and on a plugin
      source with an empty path
    * zsh's parameter flags: `${(j:,:)a[@]}`, `${(s:,:)x}`, `${(o)a[@]}`,
      `${(k)h[@]}`, and `@ v f F L U C u O i n a`
    * `${!x}` (bash): indirection, also with an operator (`${!x:-d}`)
      and to elements (`x='a[1]'`); `${!prefix@}` lists variable names
    * A lone `"${!a[@]}"` on an empty array gives no word, as `"$@"`
    * Tab after `${a[` completes the array's indices (in numeric order)
      or keys
    * `\]` is a `]` in the key of `${h[...]}`, as in `h[...]=v`
    * Plugins read and set arrays: `sh::getarray`, `sh::getmap`, and
      `sh::setvar` with an array or a map
    * `typeset -f` prints functions, `typeset +f` their names
    * `typeset -l` and `-u` (lower and upper case), and `typeset -U` (zsh:
      unique arrays, as in `typeset -U path`)
    * `typeset -i`: integer variables, as in zsh and bash
    * `typeset -p` prints special variables (`path`, `pipestatus`, `UID`)
    * `dirstack` (zsh): the directory stack as an array, which
      `dirstack=(...)` sets
    * `path` (zsh): the directories of `PATH` as an array, which
      `path=(...)` and `path+=(...)` set
    * Special variables such as `UID` are special again after a function
      with `local UID` returns, or after `UID=x cmd`
    * `[[ =~ ]]` sets `match`, `mbegin` and `mend` (zsh) and `BASH_REMATCH`
      (bash)
    * `pipestatus` (zsh) and `PIPESTATUS` (bash): the statuses of the
      last pipeline's commands
    * `${!a[@]}` gives the indices or keys of an array, as in bash
    * Associative arrays (`typeset -A`), as in zsh and bash
    * `a=([i]=x)` assigns to an index, as in zsh and bash
    * `typeset` and `declare`, as in zsh and bash
    * `read -A` (zsh) and `read -a NAME` (bash) read into an array
    * An empty array reads as unset, as in zsh and bash
    * Arrays, as in zsh and bash: `a=(x y)`, `${a[i]}`, `"${a[@]}"`, `a+=(z)`
    * `NAME+=value` appends to a variable
    * `${x:offset:length}` and `${x/pattern/replacement}`, as in zsh and bash
    * Syntax highlighting shows unset variables in a different colour
    * `[[ ... ]]` conditions, as in zsh and bash
    * `function NAME { ... }` definitions, as in zsh and bash
    * `__luish_internal check-cache` reruns the startup files to check
      their caches, which record when they were built
    * zsh's `RANDOM`, `SECONDS`, `EPOCHSECONDS`, `EPOCHREALTIME`, `UID`,
      `EUID`, `GID`, `EGID` and `HISTCMD`
    * `SHLVL` is maintained
    * `set -o pipefail`
    * `let` built-in, as in zsh
    * `shopt` (not found) suggests the matching `setopt`
    * `builtin` built-in, as in zsh and bash
    * Tab expands globs, variables and substitutions, as in zsh
    * Don't print git's error for a locked plugin commit that isn't fetched yet
    * Fix a crash on Tab after a stray `)`

Version 0.1.0 2026-09-27 by luispedro
    * First release
    * POSIX sh with dash's behaviour where POSIX is ambiguous, plus `local`
    * As fast as dash on scripts (faster on arithmetic and function calls)
    * Interactive use: zsh-style keys, syntax highlighting, autosuggestions,
      completion menu, shared history in zsh's format, zsh prompts and glob
      qualifiers, job control
    * Plugins in shell and Rhai, fetched from git and pinned in a lock file
    * Cached startup files for instant startup
    * Configuration in config.toml
    * Binaries for Linux on x86_64 and aarch64 (glibc and static musl)
